THE NEGATIVE PROJECT-FACT CLAIM'S WIRING — one field, one line of fact, in the ARBITER (the Lead's file)

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES. It tells the C++ analyzer, per file, WHETHER THAT FILE HAS A GOVERNING
MANIFEST — and nothing else. That single boolean is condition E4 of the claim's four conditions, in
`validation/m14/verification/CPP-PROJECT-FACT-ABSENT-EMITTER.md` §2, and it is what makes a claim
EMITTABLE (the evidence can see it) as opposed to UNVERIFIABLE (the evidence cannot).

WHY THE GATE IS THE ONLY PLACE THAT CAN KNOW IT. The governing manifest of a file is the NEAREST
ANCESTOR manifest, and the gate already computes exactly that: `projectFactDependenciesByFile` has an
entry for a file if and only if that file has a governing manifest, because a file with no governing
manifest gets no entry — and that absence is what the claim's rule 1 reads as UNVERIFIABLE rather
than REFUTED. The map's KEY SET is the fact, so the fact is already in the gate's hands; this patch
only carries it across.

WHY THE ANALYZER IS TOLD EXISTENCE AND NOT NAMES. The manifest's NAMES are the claim's EVIDENCE, and
the arbiter's rule is that a claim and its evidence must not come from the same author. Handing the
analyzer the name set would make the analyzer the author of both. So the analyzer receives a boolean
and the arbiter keeps the names.

WHAT IT CANNOT BREAK. The field is ADDITIVE on a context object that is already cast `as any`, and
the analyzer's emission is flag-gated (`BB_CPP_PROJECT_FACT_ABSENT=1`) and INERT without the flag, so
with the flag off this patch changes NOTHING in any run: the analyzer does not read the field unless
the flag is on, and the flag off is byte-identical by the analyzer's own contract.

THE LEAD'S OWN FILES ARE NOT TOUCHED BY THIS PATCH — it is a patch FILE, applied by the Lead in one
`git apply`, so the Lead remains the author of the change. Verified to apply cleanly against the
current HEAD with `git apply --check`.

